    ],
    "reading_order": 11,
    "text": "So, if you know what a and d are, you can list the AP. What about the other way\nround? That is, if you are given a list of numbers can you say that it is an AP and then\nfind a and d? Since a is the first term, it can easily be written. We know that in an AP,\nevery succeeding term is obtained by adding d to the preceding term. So, d found by\nsubtracting any term from its succeeding term, i.e., the term which immediately follows\nit should be same for an AP.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p005_b12",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      72.03,
      287.26,
      235.49,
      299.51
    ],
    "reading_order": 12,
    "text": "For example, for the list of numbers :",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p005_b13",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      48.03,
      304.29,
      274.08,
      367.87
    ],
    "reading_order": 13,
    "text": "6, 9, 12, 15, . . . ,\nWe have\na2 – a1 = 9  – 6 = 3,\na3 – a2 = 12 – 9 = 3,\na4 – a3 = 15 – 12 = 3",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p005_b14",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      370.05,
      420.07,
      395.51
    ],
    "reading_order": 14,
    "text": "Here the difference of any two consecutive terms in each case is 3. So, the\ngiven list is an AP whose first term a is 6 and common difference d is 3.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p005_b15",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      72.0,
      399.58,
      248.64,
      411.83
    ],
    "reading_order": 15,
    "text": "For the list of numbers : 6, 3, 0, – 3, . . .,",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p005_b16",
    "type": "formula",
    "bbox": [
      179.76,
      415.65,
      276.96,
      463.15
    ],
    "reading_order": 16,
    "text": "a2 – a1 = 3 – 6 = – 3\na3 – a2 = 0 – 3 = – 3\na4 – a3 = –3 – 0  = –3",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH105",
    "page": 5,
    "block_id": "jemh105_p005_b17",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      47.99,
